# Dependencies. No trailing slash: a worktree's `node_modules` can be a
# symlink, which `node_modules/` does not match — and an unmatched symlink is
# one `git add -A` away from being committed as a gitlink.
node_modules

# Build output
dist/

# Environment variables
# Ignore every .env variant, then re-admit the generated example. The
# previous rules listed `.env.local` and `.env.*.local` only, so an ad-hoc
# `.env.bak` or `.env.save` was stageable — a real secret one `git add -A`
# away from history.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Turborepo
.turbo/

# Next.js
.next/
# `next dev` writes here instead, so a build cannot corrupt a live dev server.
.next-dev/

# TypeScript
*.tsbuildinfo

# OS files
.DS_Store

# IDE
.idea/
.vscode/

# Test coverage
coverage/

# Playwright MCP scratch output
.playwright-mcp/

# Playwright test-runner output (#340). Distinct from .playwright-mcp/ above,
# which is the agent-driven browser's scratch directory.
test-results/
playwright-report/
blob-report/
.last-run.json

# Playwright storage state for the E2E accounts. Live session cookies —
# never commit. Regenerate with `pnpm e2e:auth`.
.auth/
# Browser-verification screenshots. They belong in the scratchpad, never in the
# tree — four of them were committed by a careless `git add -A` in #30 and cost
# 1.7MB. The repo has no legitimate PNG at its root, so this cannot hide one.
/*.png
# Same artefact, other encodings: `browser_take_screenshot` writes JPEG when asked
# for it, and on 2026-09-03 a hunter's `search-by-name-after-click.jpeg` at the
# root blocked every commit until it was accounted for.
/*.jpg
/*.jpeg
/*.webp

# Parity measurement dumps — the element geometry a parity sweep reads out of a
# frame, written to the root as `f01.json`, `f02.json` and so on. Same class of
# scratch artifact as the screenshots above. Deliberately not `/*.json`, which
# would swallow package.json and tsconfig.json.
/f[0-9][0-9].json

# The same dumps under the names sweeps actually give them once lanes are told
# to prefix scratch files with their lane number — `parity82-ours.json`,
# `frame01.json`. `f01.json` was only ever one sweep's convention, and each new
# spelling has arrived as an untracked file failing `format:check` tree-wide and
# blocking another lane's commit through the staging hook.
/parity*.json
/frame*.json

# The frame-vs-live screenshot pairs a parity sweep writes while it measures.
# `/*.png` above only reaches the root, and these land in a directory, so a
# running sweep left them untracked in the shared checkout — where the staging
# hook reads the session cwd and blocked an unrelated lane's commit.
/.parity-tmp/

# Tool-managed skill packs installed into the repo (see skills-lock.json).
# Note `.claude/skills/*`, not `.claude/skills/`: git does not descend into an
# ignored directory, so a directory rule would make the negations below
# unreachable and our own project skills could never be committed.
.claude/skills/*
!.claude/skills/run-*/
!.claude/skills/verify/
.agents/skills/
skills-lock.json

# Worktrees Claude Code creates for parallel lanes and isolated subagents.
# Their contents are a second checkout of this repo; they must never appear as
# untracked files in the main tree.
.claude/worktrees/

# Lane manifests. Runtime state for an in-flight lane, created by /orchestrate
# and deleted by /land-lanes; the durable record is the ticket tracker.
.claude/lanes/

# Overnight run logs. One directory per run, full transcripts of every lane.
.claude/logs/

# Session-local scheduler state, not configuration.
.claude/scheduled_tasks.lock

# Approved Bash commands are stored here verbatim, so this file is exactly where
# a pasted credential ends up — and this repo is public. It was protected only by
# `~/.config/git/ignore` on one machine, which no clone carries; a second machine
# would have had nothing stopping `git add -A` from publishing it.
.claude/settings.local.json

# Local design scratch, not a build input
/vendor-marketplace-header.png

# Local state written by the Neon CLI/integration
.neon

# Film assets: licensed audio/video sources and renders. These are tens of MB
# of binaries that git cannot diff, and the tracks are redownloadable from the
# artist's library. The licence receipt is the exception and stays tracked —
# it is the record of our right to use the music, and it is 4KB of text.
# Note `/uploads/*`, not `/uploads/`: git does not descend into an ignored
# directory, so a directory rule would make the negation below unreachable.
/uploads/*
!/uploads/soundtrack-license.md
# Added by `vercel link`. The `.env*` line it also appends is dropped: lines
# 12-14 already ignore every .env variant and re-admit .env.example, and a
# blanket trailing rule risks re-ignoring the generated example that must stay
# tracked.
.vercel
